+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+
+Apple Paravirtualized Graphics on Linux hosts (``apple-gfx-pci``)
+=================================================================
+
+On macOS hosts, QEMU offers the ``apple-gfx-pci`` display device backed
+by Apple's ParavirtualizedGraphics.framework. On Linux hosts, the same
+guest-visible device is available when QEMU is built against
+`libapplegfx-vulkan <https://github.com/MattJackson/libapplegfx-vulkan>`__,
+an independent implementation of the host side of the paravirtualized
+graphics protocol with a Vulkan rendering backend.
+
+An unmodified macOS guest binds its stock ``AppleParavirtGPU.kext``
+driver to the device; no guest-side changes are required. Rendering
+works on any Vulkan implementation, including Mesa's software
+``lavapipe`` driver, so no host GPU is required.
+
+Build configuration
+-------------------
+
+The device is enabled automatically when the ``libapplegfx-vulkan``
+pkg-config package is found at configure time, and can be controlled
+explicitly with ``--enable-libapplegfx`` / ``--disable-libapplegfx``.
+The configure summary reports the detection result under
+``libapplegfx support``.
+
+Usage
+-----
+
+.. code-block:: console
+
+    qemu-system-x86_64 \
+        -machine q35,accel=kvm -cpu host -m 8G -smp 4 \
+        -device apple-gfx-pci \
+        ...
+
+Properties:
+
+``display-modes``
+    Array property listing the display modes advertised to the guest,
+    each formatted as ``<width>x<height>@<refresh-rate>``, for example
+    ``-device apple-gfx-pci,display-modes.0=1920x1080@60``.
+
+``gpu_cores``
+    Caps the rendering library's worker thread pool (for Mesa lavapipe
+    this sets ``LP_NUM_THREADS``). ``0`` (the default) lets the backend
+    pick its own default, typically the host core count. Useful on
+    large hosts where the renderer would otherwise over-schedule
+    against other guest work.
+
+Limitations
+-----------
+
+* Live migration is not supported; the device registers a migration
+  blocker.
+* The device provides no VGA or UEFI GOP compatibility surface, so
+  firmware and early-boot output are not displayed; the guest's
+  paravirtualized graphics driver must initialize before pixels
+  appear. Combine with a secondary VGA device if firmware output is
+  needed.
